WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guest is a talented
actor and comedian

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.671
you know from his work on
"Saturday Night Live"

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:06.472
and "30 Rock" and animated shows

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:08.107
such as "Archer"
and "Rick and Morty."

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:11.477
He stars in "Senior Year", which
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:12.812
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.914
-You want a beer?

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:17.684
-Oh, uh...sure.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.186
-Bah! Wrong answer, hot rod.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.854
You think I'm gonna let you
drink and drive

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:23.156
with my daughter?

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:24.924
-Oh, well,
I'm actually not driving.

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:25.991
We're taking a Lyft.

00:00:25.991 --> 00:00:28.260
-Oh, cool.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.564
Though I suspect Stephanie would
prefer not to spend her prom

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.433
evening with a drunken fool.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:34.734
-Well, it'll just be the --

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.502
Uh, no, I think I'm okay,
actually.

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:38.004
Maybe have a water if anything.

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:41.340
-Did you bring some condoms?
-[ Gulps ]

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.108
Um, no.

00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:45.878
-Hmm. Didn't think we need to
have this talk.

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:49.415
But, uh, Seth, you are aware
that unprotected sex

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.384
can lead to
geriatric pregnancies?

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.419
-Stephanie --
-You ready to be an old dad?

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:55.988
-I'm sorry. Do you want me to
sleep with your daughter or --

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:57.890
-What did you just ask me?

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.125
-Please welcome to the show
our good friend

00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:01.561
Chris Parnell, everybody!

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.763
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:09.602 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:15.474 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:17.577
Welcome. It's so wonderful
to have you here.

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:18.911
-Thank you.
It's wonderful to be here.

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:21.214
-Yeah, back at 30 Rock,
where we first met.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:24.350
This is a fantastic film with
Rebel Wilson.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.687
She plays a woman who's come out
of a 20-year coma

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:29.522
and is now going through
her senior year.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:30.990
-Yes, she's determined
to go back

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.591
and finish out her senior year.

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:33.960
-And you play her father.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.495
And had you ever worked with
Rebel before?

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:36.895
She's sort of a force of nature.

00:01:36.895 --> 00:01:38.431
-I hadn't, but she is.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.501
But she's also just like a cool,
normal, real person, you know?

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.803
But she's so funny.
I mean, some of the stuff --

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.572
When we were shooting,
there's a scene where I take

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:50.676
her phone away, and she fights
it very vehemently.

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:52.912
And when we were shooting it,
I thought to myself, "That was

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:55.582
probably a little over the top."
I just kept it to myself.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:56.882
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:58.250
I was like, "Eh, that's too
much, that's too much.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.986
Take it down a notch."

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.654
But then when I saw it, I was
like, "Oh, no, she was right.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:02.888
That's brilliant."
-She knows what she's doing.

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:04.257
-It's hilarious. Yeah.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:05.892
-You actually taught high school

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:09.095
for a year after --
after college you went back?

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:10.696
-Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:12.231
-I'm assuming
you taught theater.

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:15.167
-I did. I taught at my old high
school, Germantown High School.

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:16.603
Taught introduction to theater

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:18.004
and introduction to
film and video.

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:21.273
-Gotcha.
-Classes I had taken.

00:02:21.273 --> 00:02:23.109
And yeah, I mean, I loved
working with all the kids who

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:25.077
were involved with the theater
department and the TV studio

00:02:25.077 --> 00:02:26.312
because that's what I did,
and those are the really

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:27.647
dedicated students.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:30.116
I wasn't so cut out
for the day-to-day teaching.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.317
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.354
-Does that mean there were kids
in your class --

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:36.522
some of your classes, who maybe
weren't as invested in it?

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:38.024
-Exactly.
-Yeah.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.426
-Yeah, it was like there would
be a group of students who are

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.528
very dedicated just 'cause they
wanted good grades

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:44.129
or whatever --
they were serious students.

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:45.932
Then there's the sort of the
bulk of them that were like,

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.234
"Oh, okay, I'll kinda try."
And then there are a few that

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.436
are just, like, determined to
make my life hell, you know?

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.171
[ Laughter ]
-And do you think --

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:54.540
'Cause that's a young age
to be a teacher.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.775
I would assume you were kind of
like blood in the water.

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:59.011
They knew they had something.
-It's true, very much.

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:00.779
And I floundered.

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:04.016
Much like now, I have no idea
how to maintain discipline

00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:05.985
with my own kids.
I certainly didn't then.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:09.021
[ Laughter ]
-And then you --

00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:13.625
Sort of your path to
your comedy career was to L.A.

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:14.694
and then you did
the Groundlings,

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.830
a very famous comedy theater
in L.A.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:22.501
And you actually were roommates
with a pre-"SNL" Kristen Wiig?

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:23.636
-That's right, yeah.

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.238
-How did you guys end up
as roommates?

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:28.174
-Her roommate at the time,
Holly Mandel, was coming to

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.843
New York for a bit, and I was
going to L.A. for a bit,

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:33.879
so Holly stayed in my place
and I stayed in her bedroom.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:37.116
-And how quickly did you --
Could you have any sense of

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:39.586
those days of like, "Oh, my God,
Kristen Wiig is something else"?

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:41.988
-Well, I mean,
she was pretty funny.

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:43.389
-Yeah.
-She was pretty fun.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.324
It was completely platonic.

00:03:45.324 --> 00:03:46.526
But, yeah, it was just a fun --

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:49.428
it was a fun few months
or whatever.

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:51.430
I remember taking pictures of
each other

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.233
under the kitchen cabinets,
like hiding in there.

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:55.401
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.903
-That's a very --
I will say that's a very --

00:03:57.903 --> 00:03:59.205
For anybody knows Kristen Wiig,

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.407
that's a very Kristen Wiig
thing, that she would get

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:03.609
an idea of like, "Here's we're
gonna do tonight.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:05.077
We're gonna take pictures that
make it look like

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:06.546
we're in the kitchen cabinets."
-Right.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.647
-And it would be
like an eight-hour bit, yeah.

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:10.282
-But we really were
in the kitchen cabinets.

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:12.452
-Oh, there you go.
Yeah, you guys never cheated it.

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.786
That was the thing about
you guys.
-No, that was it.

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:16.255
-You had a nickname at "SNL"
I remember hearing

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.357
when I showed up,
which was "Ice Man,"

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:21.661
which both referred to the fact
that you would execute a script

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.663
sort of word perfectly, no
flubs, but also you never --

00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:28.467
You famously never broken
sketches, including maybe

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.837
what is the hardest to believe
of all time --

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:34.673
You were in "Cowbell" and were
the only member of "Cowbell"

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:36.041
who kept it together.

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:37.477
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:39.812
-I mean, that's really, like,
that's the gold standard of

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:42.415
not breaking because I think,
when you go back and watch it,

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:44.917
it seems like Will's sole
purpose in that sketch

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:47.052
was to make his castmates break.
-I think it was.

00:04:47.052 --> 00:04:48.087
I think it was. Yeah.

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.856
Christopher Walken and I
did not break.

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:51.524
-That's right.
-Although there was a moment

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:54.359
that the camera was not on me,
and I knew it wasn't on me,

00:04:54.359 --> 00:04:56.729
and I looked down at my guitar,
and I smiled.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.497
So I don't know if that counts
as a break.

00:04:58.497 --> 00:05:01.067
-That, like, speaks to your
nobility as a performer that

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:03.502
you want to let us know like,
"I wasn't perfect off-camera."

00:05:03.502 --> 00:05:05.170
-I wasn't.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.573
I'm not really the Ice Man.
-It's weird.

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:10.509
It never even occurred to me
that Christopher Walken

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:13.045
also didn't break 'cause just
the idea of Christopher Walken

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:14.680
breaking is, like,
so crazy to me.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.115
-It's impossible.
-Yeah.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.683
'Cause I, in my head, he just --

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:20.152
That whole sketch
is that character.

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:22.020
He's never a guy who's, like,
judging whether or not

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.990
this is a funny sketch.
-No, he's right there.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.593
He's in it. He is that guy.

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:31.697
-You've been doing a lot of V.O.
work as well.

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:35.034
"Archer."
Another season of "Archer"?

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:38.137
-We're waiting to hear,
you know. We got season 13

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:40.706
that I don't know
if that's about to come out.

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:42.708
I kind of lose track.
-And "Rick and Morty" as well.

00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:44.110
-"Rick and Morty."
[ Audience cheers ]

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.145
-There we go.
It must be wonderful,

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:48.681
for both those shows,
when the scripts come in,

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:50.649
because they do -- certainly
from watching them,

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:52.585
it seems like an incredible
amount of care and love

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:54.286
goes into those scripts.
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:55.721
They're very dense.

00:05:55.721 --> 00:05:58.223
You know, even though the show
doesn't last that long,

00:05:58.223 --> 00:06:00.526
you know, it's like a lot of
material in there,

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:01.694
and there are a lot of jokes.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:05.231
And, yeah, they're delightful
to read.

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:07.200
-Like, I mean,
I have always thought

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.601
you have a million-dollar voice.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.870
Have you finally moved
to the position

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.838
that you have
a great voice-over voice?

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:14.306
-Well, you know what
the thing is?

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:17.076
A lot of voice-overs now,
they don't necessarily want

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:20.012
a voice-over voice guy, you
know, unless you're doing promos

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:23.382
or something like that,
so a lot of the commercial work

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:26.319
is about just sounding like
a normal guy, whatever that is.

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:28.220
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.955
-Coming out of you,
it doesn't look normal at all.

00:06:29.955 --> 00:06:32.391
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:35.661
You know, we did some sketch
work together, not a lot, but we

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:38.764
were in "Appalachian Emergency
Room" written by Paula Pell.

00:06:38.764 --> 00:06:40.032
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.434
You played a character
named Tyler.

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:43.803
-That's right.
-And Tyler often was in

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:47.005
the Appalachian emergency room
for a pretty specific reason.

00:06:47.005 --> 00:06:49.875
-Yeah. He got a lot of things
stuck up

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:52.144
in his rear behind area.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:54.980
-And often kind of went out of
his way to explain

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.482
how it had been
a total accident.

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:58.150
-It was a very elaborate
explanation.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.387
-Yeah. But not particularly easy
to believe, but elaborate.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.289
-Never.
-We talk about Second Chance

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:06.858
Theatre here with my old "SNL"
castmates, something they did

00:07:06.858 --> 00:07:09.195
that maybe fell a little flat
in front of the audience.

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:12.631
-Right.
-Of your work, what would you

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:14.966
propose to get the Second Chance
Theatre treatment?

00:07:14.966 --> 00:07:17.003
-Oh, my goodness, wow.

00:07:17.003 --> 00:07:20.539
There definitely were some "SNL"
sketches that I think I could

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:23.008
have bumped up a little better.

00:07:23.008 --> 00:07:25.644
There was one I did
for Andrew Steele.

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:27.079
Andrew Steele wrote it.
-Yep.

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:30.149
-And it was Steve Baxter,
Hollywood gynecologist.

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:31.550
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:34.219
And Bill Murray was the host.
He was the gynecologist.

00:07:34.219 --> 00:07:38.224
And I was, like, a jazzy lounge
singer guy singing about him.

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:39.358
And there's one moment in it

00:07:39.358 --> 00:07:41.494
where it was just kind of
a musical break.

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:45.531
And I wanted to do this thing,
like, I'd be like, "Ooh-ooh-ah,"

00:07:45.531 --> 00:07:47.466
a guy really feeling the music
and all that.

00:07:47.466 --> 00:07:49.402
And Lorne,
between dress and air, told me,

00:07:49.402 --> 00:07:51.203
"Don't do that thing
with your head."

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:53.005
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:54.740
And I remember being tormented
by it.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:55.974
I was talking to Tina Fey.

00:07:55.974 --> 00:07:57.944
I was like, "But that's my best
moment in it."

00:07:57.944 --> 00:08:00.179
And so I ended up doing it.

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:01.213
[ Laughter ]

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:03.182
And I think it was a mistake.

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:04.816
[ Laughter ]

00:08:04.816 --> 00:08:06.451
-Well, you know what?

00:08:06.451 --> 00:08:09.155
It's nice that you can look back
and have that judgment.

00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:10.590
Yeah, yeah.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.658
Every now and then, we make
the wrong call in the moment.

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:14.360
-You got to listen to Lorne.
-You got to listen to Lorne.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.028
Hey, congratulations
on the film.

00:08:16.028 --> 00:08:17.296
Thanks so much for being here.
It's always so great to see you.

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:18.931
-Thanks for having me.
-Chris Parnell, everybody.

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:20.900
"Senior Year" is currently
streaming on Netflix.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.870
We'll be right back with
Kwame Onwuachi.

